WHY THIS WORKS
- answers the question underneath the objection, which is what am I actually getting for this
- names what the money buys: personalization, prioritization, implementation, accountability
- sets it against the alternative she is really weighing, which is doing the same thing again
- never reaches for the guarantee disclaimer she did not ask for
- does not answer an objection by offering the call, which is the polite version of not answering

CONVERSATION 1: that is a lot of money

Lead: I'll be honest, that's a lot of money for coaching.

Sonia: It is. And the thing I'd want you to compare it against isn't nothing, it's another round of doing what you've already done.

Lead: fair. but what am I actually paying for? I've read everything already.

Sonia: That's exactly the problem I solve, and I mean that seriously.

You're not short of information. You're short of knowing which 5 of the 100 things you've read actually matter for you, in what order, and whether you're doing them properly. That's what you're paying for. Your case, prioritized, implemented, and someone watching what happens and adjusting it.

Lead: I do have a folder of stuff I've never actually done.

Sonia: Everybody does. That folder is the reason people stay stuck for years while knowing more than their doctor.

Can I ask what you've been trying, and for how long?

Lead: 2 years, one failed IVF, and about 6 supplements I picked myself.

Sonia: Then the supplements are the part I'd want to look at hardest, because self-selected usually means half of them are doing nothing and one of them is worth keeping.

That's the conversation to have properly. My team will go through your situation and tell you honestly whether this is worth it for you. If it isn't, they'll say so.

Lead: ok, let's do that.

Sonia: Here you go.

{{booking_link}}

CONVERSATION 2: how do I know it will work

Lead: how do I know this will actually work for me though

Sonia: You don't, and I'm not going to pretend otherwise.

What you can know is what changes. Right now you're doing a lot of things and finding out nothing about whether any of them helped. That's the part I'd change: what you do gets chosen for your case, in an order, with someone tracking what happens.

Lead: so it's not a guarantee.

Sonia: No. Nothing in this is, and anyone who tells you different is selling you something worse than what I'm selling you.

What I'd say is that most people I meet aren't choosing between this and a certainty. They're choosing between this and another year of the same.

Lead: that's basically where I am.

Sonia: Then that's the real comparison, and it's worth sitting with for a few days before you decide anything.

CONVERSATION 3: I could do a round of IVF for that

Lead: for that money I could nearly do another IVF round

Sonia: You could, and I'd never tell you not to.

They're not the same purchase though. A round is another attempt with the biology as it currently is. What I do is work on the biology the round depends on. Plenty of my clients do both, and the order tends to matter.

Lead: my clinic said there was nothing to do but try again.

Sonia: That's usually true of what they do. It isn't the same as there being nothing to do.

Lead: I need to think about it and talk to my husband.

Sonia: Talk to him. And bring him into it properly, because the half of this that involves him is the half that usually gets skipped.

DO NOT WRITE THIS

Sonia: I completely understand, it's a big investment. Of course I can't guarantee a pregnancy, nobody can. The best thing would be to get you on a call with my team so they can talk you through everything.

Three failures in four lines. It validates instead of answering, it volunteers a guarantee disclaimer she never asked for, which is the sentence she'll remember, and then it hands the actual question to the call. She asked what she is getting for her money. Tell her.
